[
    [
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "15 Set Cover via the Primal–Dual Schema"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 1
            },
            "bbox": [
                223,
                109,
                737,
                128
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "As noted in Section 12.3, the primal–dual schema is the method of choice for designing approximation algorithms since it yields combinatorial algorithms with good approximation factors and good running times. We will first present the central ideas behind this schema and then use it to design a simple f factor algorithm for set cover, where f is the frequency of the most frequent element."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                220,
                255,
                786,
                340
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "The primal–dual schema has its origins in the design of exact algorithms. In that setting, this schema yielded the most eficient algorithms for some of the cornerstone problems in P, including matching, network flow, and shortest paths. These problems have the property that their LP-relaxations have integral optimal solutions. By Theorem 12.3 we know that optimal solutions to linear programs are characterized by fact that they satisfy all the complementary slackness conditions. In fact, the primal–dual schema for exact algorithms is driven by these conditions. Starting with initial feasible solutions to the primal and dual programs, it iteratively starts satisfying complementary slackness conditions. When they are all satisfied, both solutions must be optimal. During the iterations, the primal is always modified integrally, so that eventually we get an integral optimal solution."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                220,
                340,
                786,
                512
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Consider an LP-relaxation for an NP-hard problem. In general, the relaxation will not have an optimal solution that is integral. Does this rule out a complementary slackness condition driven approach? Interestingly enough, the answer is ‘no’. It turns out that the algorithm can be driven by a suitable relaxation of these conditions! This is the most commonly used way of designing primal–dual based approximation algorithms – but not the only way."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                220,
                512,
                786,
                611
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "15.1 Overview of the schema"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                223,
                640,
                519,
                655
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Let us consider the following primal program, written in standard form."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                670,
                754,
                684
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "equation_interline",
            "content": {
                "math_content": "\\text { minimize } \\quad \\sum_ {j = 1} ^ {n} c _ {j} x _ {j}",
                "math_type": "latex",
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/fa89d0a73517b55783d793dd57a535353d37809eabf5fdeeaa68580e60307e86.jpg"
                }
            },
            "bbox": [
                263,
                697,
                423,
                736
            ]
        }
    ],
    [
        {
            "type": "equation_interline",
            "content": {
                "math_content": "\\begin{array}{l l} \\text { subject   to } & \\sum_ {j = 1} ^ {n} a _ {i j} x _ {j} \\geq b _ {i}, \\quad i = 1, \\ldots , m \\\\ & x _ {j} \\geq 0, \\quad j = 1, \\ldots , n \\end{array}",
                "math_type": "latex",
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/e26b8223efe1dcf977f80b37843696c77bd37b43dce656ddd80dd9eff61ea1d6.jpg"
                }
            },
            "bbox": [
                262,
                110,
                586,
                166
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "where "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "a _ { i j } , b _ { i } ,"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", and "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "c _ { j }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "are specified in the input. The dual program is:"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                220,
                177,
                729,
                193
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "equation_interline",
            "content": {
                "math_content": "\\begin{array}{l l} \\text {maximize} & \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {m} b _ {i} y _ {i} \\\\ \\text {subject to} & \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {m} a _ {i j} y _ {i} \\leq c _ {j}, \\quad j = 1, \\ldots , n \\\\ & y _ {i} \\geq 0, \\quad i = 1, \\ldots , m \\end{array}",
                "math_type": "latex",
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/20e8197c7064a8530e9f386f6801c1068ad8b2f8ece06dfaffb9cb2f84345656.jpg"
                }
            },
            "bbox": [
                260,
                205,
                583,
                304
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Most known approximation algorithms using the primal–dual schema run by ensuring one set of conditions and suitably relaxing the other. In the following description we capture both situations by relaxing both conditions. Eventually, if primal conditions are ensured, we set "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\alpha = 1"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", and if dual conditions are ensured, we set "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\beta = 1"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                220,
                313,
                786,
                387
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Primal complementary slackness conditions"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                394,
                594,
                407
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Let "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\alpha \\geq 1 ."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                248,
                407,
                331,
                420
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "For each "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\begin{array} { r } { 1 \\leq j \\leq n \\colon \\mathrm { e i t h e r } \\ x _ { j } = 0 \\ \\mathrm { o r } \\ c _ { j } / \\alpha \\leq \\sum _ { i = 1 } ^ { m } a _ { i j } y _ { i } \\leq c _ { j } . } \\end{array}"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                248,
                420,
                700,
                438
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Dual complementary slackness conditions"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                444,
                578,
                458
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Let "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\beta \\geq 1"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                248,
                458,
                331,
                472
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "For each "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "1 \\leq i \\leq m \\colon"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "either "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\begin{array} { r } { y _ { i } = 0 \\mathrm { ~ o r ~ } b _ { i } \\le \\sum _ { j = 1 } ^ { n } a _ { i j } x _ { j } \\le \\beta \\cdot b _ { i } , } \\end{array}"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                248,
                472,
                704,
                489
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Proposition 15.1 If x and y are primal and dual feasible solutions satisfying the conditions stated above then"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                495,
                784,
                524
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "equation_interline",
            "content": {
                "math_content": "\\sum_ {j = 1} ^ {n} c _ {j} x _ {j} \\leq \\alpha \\cdot \\beta \\cdot \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {m} b _ {i} y _ {i}.",
                "math_type": "latex",
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/ff5a5f218df343da449c0bd1e553794e0d09a578e72fb61c5807ab180d16ddff.jpg"
                }
            },
            "bbox": [
                260,
                533,
                453,
                573
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Proof:"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                590,
                280,
                602
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "equation_interline",
            "content": {
                "math_content": "\\begin{array}{r l} & {\\sum_ {j = 1} ^ {n} c _ {j} x _ {j} \\leq \\alpha \\sum_ {j = 1} ^ {n} \\left(\\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {m} a _ {i j} y _ {i}\\right) x _ {j} = \\alpha \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {m} \\left(\\sum_ {j = 1} ^ {n} a _ {i j} x _ {j}\\right) y _ {i}} \\\\ & {\\qquad \\leq \\alpha \\beta \\sum_ {i = 1} ^ {m} b _ {i} y _ {i}.} \\end{array}\\tag{15.1}",
                "math_type": "latex",
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/3c920dcca5c3a071811891d743cf6263780c0a331a748a2ef7e907c4629c0b20.jpg"
                }
            },
            "bbox": [
                260,
                615,
                668,
                697
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "The first and second inequalities follow from the primal and dual conditions, respectively. The equality follows by simply changing the order of summation. ✷"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                220,
                709,
                784,
                752
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_number",
            "content": {
                "page_number_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "126"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                225,
                87,
                250,
                99
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_header",
            "content": {
                "page_header_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "15 Set Cover via the Primal–Dual Schema"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                277,
                87,
                579,
                101
            ]
        }
    ],
    [
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "The algorithm starts with a primal infeasible solution and a dual feasible solution; these are usually the trivial solutions "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "{ \\pmb x } = { \\bf 0 }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "and "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { \\nabla } _ { \\mathbf { y } } = \\mathbf { 0 }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ". It iteratively improves the feasibility of the primal solution, and the optimality of the dual solution, ensuring that in the end a primal feasible solution is obtained and all conditions stated above, with a suitable choice of "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\alpha"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "and "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\beta ,"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "are satisfied. The primal solution is always extended integrally, thus ensuring that the final solution is integral. The improvements to the primal and the dual "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "_ \\mathrm { g o }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "handin-hand: the current primal solution is used to determine the improvement to the dual, and vice versa. Finally, the cost of the dual solution is used as a lower bound on "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathrm { O P T }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", and by Proposition 15.1, the approximation guarantee of the algorithm is "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\alpha \\beta"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                220,
                112,
                786,
                271
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "15.2 Primal–dual schema applied to set cover"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                299,
                685,
                315
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Let us obtain a factor "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "f"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "algorithm for the set cover problem using the primal– dual schema. For this algorithm, we will choose "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\alpha = 1"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "and "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\beta = f"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ". We will work with the primal and dual pair of "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathrm { L P } { \\mathrm { { s } } }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "given in (13.2) and (13.3), respectively. The complementary slackness conditions are:"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                220,
                329,
                784,
                386
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Primal conditions"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                393,
                378,
                407
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "equation_interline",
            "content": {
                "math_content": "\\forall S \\in \\mathcal {S}: x _ {S} \\neq 0 \\Rightarrow \\sum_ {e: e \\in S} y _ {e} = c (S).",
                "math_type": "latex",
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/2ca03f3c275c328ba76d7d9ecebe500bf6d2def07f795d7459cc5a764172763c.jpg"
                }
            },
            "bbox": [
                262,
                417,
                527,
                449
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Set "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "S"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "will be said to be tight if "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\begin{array} { r } { \\sum _ { e \\colon { e \\in S } } y _ { e } = c ( S ) } \\end{array}"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ". Since we will increment \u0001the primal variables integrally, we can state the conditions as: Pick only tight sets in the cover."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                461,
                784,
                502
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Clearly, in order to maintain dual feasibility, we are not allowed to overpack any set."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                504,
                784,
                532
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Dual conditions"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                539,
                361,
                552
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "equation_interline",
            "content": {
                "math_content": "\\forall e: y _ {e} \\neq 0 \\Rightarrow \\sum_ {S: e \\in S} x _ {S} \\leq f",
                "math_type": "latex",
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/b66d80cfd8b1ec7bb7610400b8d54449f98b9945f7938258d52c6286f45b5c83.jpg"
                }
            },
            "bbox": [
                260,
                565,
                470,
                595
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Since we will find a "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "0 / 1"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "solution for "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "{ \\mathbf { } } ^ { \\mathbf { } } \\mathbf { { \\mathbf { x } } } ,"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "these conditions are equivalent to: Each element having a nonzero dual value can be covered at most "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "f"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "times. Since each element is in at most "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "f"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "sets, this condition is trivially satisfied for all elements."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                221,
                608,
                784,
                664
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "The two sets of conditions naturally suggest the following algorithm:"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                245,
                665,
                756,
                680
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_header",
            "content": {
                "page_header_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "15.2 Primal–dual schema applied to set cover"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                408,
                87,
                731,
                101
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_number",
            "content": {
                "page_number_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "127"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                759,
                87,
