    [
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": []
            },
            "bbox": [
                220,
                112,
                786,
                156
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Algorithm 15.2 is due to Bar-Yehuda and Even [20]. Although it was not originally stated as a primal–dual algorithm, in retrospect, this was the first use of the schema in approximation algorithms. The works of Agrawal, Klein, and Ravi [1] and Goemans and Williamson [105] revived the use of this schema in the latter setting, and introduced the powerful idea of growing duals in a synchronized manner (see Chapter 22). The mechanism of relaxing complementary slackness conditions was first formalized in Williamson, Goemans, Mihail, and Vazirani [258]. For further historical information, see Goemans and Williamson [107]."
                    }
                ]
            },
